  "answers": {
    "traditional_rag": "**Direct mechanistic evidence**\n\n* **Dysbiosis ➜ periodontitis** – Oral pathobionts expand in dysbiotic biofilms and, in mouse models, this community shift initiates periodontal inflammation and bone loss (animal evidence)【PMID:25574929】. Metatranscriptomic profiling of progressing sites shows that metabolic changes characteristic of dysbiosis (e.g., lipid‑A, iron‑transport, protease up‑regulation) precede clinical disease, indicating a causal link【PMID:25918553】.  \n\n* **Periodontitis ➜ local inflammation** – Human periodontal lesions harbor keystone pathogens (e.g., *Porphyromonas gingivalis*) that provoke strong innate immune activation; in vitro macrophage studies demonstrate reduced LXR expression and heightened cytokine release (IL‑1β, TNF‑α) when challenged with these bacteria【PMID:25946408】. The “inflammophilic” nature of the dysbiotic community further amplifies the host inflammatory response【PMID:24976068】.  \n\n* **Inflammation ➜ alveolar bone loss** – Animal experiments identify IL‑1, TNF, prostaglandins, complement and RANKL as essential mediators that translate periodontal inflammation into osteoclast‑driven bone resorption【PMID:25574929】. LXR‑deficient mice experience greater *P. gingivalis*‑induced cytokine production and exaggerated oral bone loss, confirming a mechanistic role for inflammatory signaling pathways【PMID:25946408】.  \n\n* **Inflammation (systemic) ➜ osteoporosis** – Epidemiologic reviews of post‑menopausal women reveal"
